# Every tracked file in this repository is UTF-8 text, stored with LF, and
# checked out with LF on every platform. The catch-all below is what makes that
# true for a file type nobody has added yet, which is the case a list of
# extensions gets wrong.
#
# tools/text-guard.py refuses a stored carriage return, a leading byte order
# mark, bytes that are not UTF-8, a missing final newline, a tracked path this
# file does not reach, and a path that would be checked out with anything but
# LF. It reads the bytes git holds rather than the bytes in the working tree,
# so a clone configured to convert line endings is judged on what it would
# push rather than on what it checked out.
#
# A fixture that has to carry a byte the normalisation would remove is not
# exempted here. It is stored encoded, in base64 in the source of the test that
# needs it, so that the bytes reaching the code under test are exact and no
# checkout setting can repair them on the way in. Turning normalisation off for
# a path instead is possible and needs the reason on the line above the
# pattern, which the guard refuses a pattern without. There is no such pattern
# in this file today.

* text=auto eol=lf
